#, fuzzy, python-format
#~| msgid ""
#~| "\n"
#~| "          <p>We use a number of terms to describe software available on "
#~| "PyPI, like \"project\", \"release\", \"file\", and \"package\". "
#~| "Sometimes those terms are confusing because they're used to describe "
#~| "different things in other contexts. Here's how we use them on PyPI:</p>\n"
#~| "          <p>A \"project\" on PyPI is the name of a collection of "
#~| "releases and files, and information about them. Projects on PyPI are "
#~| "made and shared by other members of the Python community so that you can "
#~| "use them.</p>\n"
#~| "          <p>A \"release\" on PyPI is a specific version of a project. "
#~| "For example, the <a href=\"%(requests_href)s\">requests</a> project has "
#~| "many releases, like \"requests 2.10\" and \"requests 1.2.1\". A release "
#~| "consists of one or more \"files\".</p>\n"
#~| "          <p>A \"file\", also known as a \"package\", on PyPI is "
#~| "something that you can download and install. Because of different "
#~| "hardware, operating systems, and file formats, a release may have "
#~| "several files (packages), like an archive containing source code or a "
#~| "binary <a href=\"%(wheel_href)s\">wheel</a>.</p>\n"
#~| "        "
#~ msgid ""
#~ "<p> We use a number of terms to describe software available on PyPI, like "
#~ "\"project\", \"release\", \"file\", and \"package\". Sometimes those "
#~ "terms are confusing because they're used to describe different things in "
#~ "other contexts. Here's how we use them on PyPI: </p> <p> A \"project\" on "
#~ "PyPI is the name of a collection of releases and files, and information "
#~ "about them. Projects on PyPI are made and shared by other members of the "
#~ "Python community so that you can use them. </p> <p> A \"release\" on PyPI "
#~ "is a specific version of a project. For example, the <a "
#~ "href=\"%(requests_href)s\">requests</a> project has many releases, like "
#~ "\"requests 2.10\" and \"requests 1.2.1\". A release consists of one or "
#~ "more \"files\". </p> <p> A \"file\", also known as a \"package\", on PyPI "
#~ "is something that you can download and install. Because of different "
#~ "hardware, operating systems, and file formats, a release may have several "
#~ "files (packages), like an archive containing source code or a binary <a "
#~ "href=\"%(wheel_href)s\">wheel</a>. </p>"
#~ msgstr ""
#~ "\n"
#~ "          <p>Kami menggunakan sejumlah istilah untuk mendeskripsikan "
#~ "perangkat lunak yang tersedia di PyPI, seperti \"proyek\", \"rilis\", "
#~ "\"file\", dan \"paket\". Terkadang istilah-istilah itu membingungkan "
#~ "karena digunakan untuk menggambarkan hal yang berbeda dalam konteks lain. "
#~ "Inilah cara kami menggunakannya di PyPI:</p>\n"
#~ "          <p>Sebuah \"proyek\" di PyPI adalah nama kumpulan rilis dan "
#~ "file, serta informasi tentangnya. Proyek di PyPI dibuat dan dibagikan "
#~ "oleh anggota komunitas Python lainnya sehingga Anda dapat menggunakannya."
#~ "</p>\n"
#~ "          <p>Sebuah \"rilis\" di PyPI adalah versi tertentu dari sebuah "
#~ "proyek. Misalnya, proyek <a href=\"%(requests_href)s\">requests</a> "
#~ "memiliki banyak rilis, seperti \"request 2.10\" dan \"request 1.2.1\". "
#~ "Sebuah rilis terdiri dari satu atau lebih \"file\".</p>\n"
#~ "          <p>Sebuah \"file\", juga dikenal sebagai \"paket\", di PyPI "
#~ "adalah sesuatu yang dapat Anda unduh dan instal. Karena perangkat keras, "
#~ "sistem operasi, dan format file yang berbeda, rilis mungkin memiliki "
#~ "beberapa file (paket), seperti arsip yang berisi kode sumber or a binary "
#~ "<a href=\"%(wheel_href)s\">wheel</a>.</p>\n"
#~ "        "

